**Simplest correct approach:** convert the AnimatedImageDrawable to an ImageBitmap each time it's invalidated, using a `Drawable.Callback` that triggers recomposition. Compose has `drawable.toBitmap()` from `androidx.core.graphics.drawable.toBitmap` (androidx.core-ktx is a dependency!). So:

```kotlin
val bitmap = remember(drawable) { mutableStateOf<ImageBitmap?>(null) }
DisposableEffect(drawable) {
    val cb = object : Drawable.Callback {
        override fun invalidateDrawable(who: Drawable) { bitmap.value = drawable.toBitmap().asImageBitmap() }
        override fun scheduleDrawable(who: Drawable, what: Runnable) {}
        override fun unscheduleDrawable(who: Drawable, what: Runnable) {}
    }
    drawable.callback = cb
    drawable.start()
    bitmap.value = drawable.toBitmap().asImageBitmap()
    onDispose { drawable.stop(); drawable.callback = null }
}
bitmap.value?.let { Image(it, ...) }
```

`toBitmap()` allocates a new bitmap per frame — at GIF sizes (say 480×480) that's ~900KB per frame at 20fps = GC churn. Not great but workable if I cap the size. Actually AnimatedImageDrawable is mutable and shared, so drawing it while it advances is fine visually.

